# 7. Payment Architecture

Do not hard-code Privy as the only possible payment method.

Use a Payment Router abstraction.

Conceptual interface:

```ts
export interface PaymentProvider {
  canPay(request: PaymentRequest): Promise<boolean>;

  pay(
    request: PaymentRequest,
    authorization: PaymentAuthorization
  ): Promise<PaymentResult>;
}
```

Possible providers:
- PrivyPaymentProvider
- StripePaymentProvider
- AlipayPaymentProvider
- WeChatPayProvider
- BankPaymentProvider

For the MVP, Privy may be the only implemented wallet provider, but the architecture must remain provider-agnostic.

---

# 8. AI Payment Safety Model

Never give the LLM unrestricted access to private keys or arbitrary fund movement.

Required execution path:

AI
  ↓
Create / request PaymentRequest
  ↓
Policy Engine
  ↓
Authorization
  ↓
Payment Provider
  ↓
Blockchain / payment rail

The AI can propose actions.

The policy layer decides whether the action:
- can auto-execute
- requires confirmation
- must be blocked

Example policy rules:
- max automatic amount per transaction
- max automatic amount per day
- allowed merchants
- allowed payment categories
- allowed chains
- allowed assets
- allowed recipient types
- transaction expiration
- user confirmation requirements

Example:

<= 50 USDC
→ may auto-pay if merchant and category are whitelisted

50–1000 USDC
→ require explicit user confirmation

> 1000 USDC
→ block unless elevated authorization exists

Never let prompt content override payment policy.

---

# 9. A2A / Agent Architecture

A2A is the communication layer between agents.

Example:

User
  ↓
Our AI Agent
  ↓
A2A Client
  ↓
Travel / Merchant Agent
  ↓
Quote / Order / Payment Request
  ↓
Our Payment Router
  ↓
Privy or another provider

The external agent must never directly control the user's wallet.

External agent responses should be treated as untrusted input.

Validate:
- merchant identity
- amount
- currency
- asset
- chain
- recipient
- expiration
- order ID
- payment terms

before producing a PaymentRequest.

---

# 10. MCP Architecture

MCP is for tool access.

Suggested structure:

AI Agent
  ├── MCP → viem / internal tools
  ├── MCP → internal tools
  ├── A2A → external agents
  └── API → internal Hono routes

Expose the smallest tool surface possible.

Prefer purpose-specific tools such as:
- getWalletBalance
- getWalletTransactions
- createPaymentRequest
- requestPaymentAuthorization
- executeAuthorizedPayment
- getOrder
- callTravelAgent

Avoid exposing generic unrestricted "sendTransaction" capabilities directly to an LLM unless a policy layer sits in front of it.

---

# 11. Wallet Layer

Do not import Privy throughout every React component.

Create an abstraction layer.

Suggested structure:

src/
  web3/
    client.ts
    wallet.ts
    accounts.ts
    balances.ts
    transactions.ts
    contracts.ts
    chains.ts

Components should call application-level functions such as:

```ts
wallet.getBalance()
wallet.sendToken()
wallet.getAddress()
wallet.prepareTransaction()
```

instead of depending directly on Privy primitives everywhere.

This reduces vendor lock-in.

---

# 12. State Management

Use TanStack Query for server state:
- balances
- token lists
- transaction history
- orders
- agent tasks
- payment requests
- payment status

Use Zustand only for client/UI state:
- selected chain
- selected wallet
- active dialog
- send flow draft
- AI session UI state
- temporary confirmation state

Do not store canonical server data in Zustand.

